      "repeat": 1,
      "seed": 42,
      "messages": [
        {
          "role": "user",
          "content": "Can I respond to my opponent tapping a basic Forest for mana?"
        }
      ],
      "card_ids": [],
      "answer": "Ruling: No. You cannot respond to your opponent tapping a basic Forest for mana.\n\nWhy: A basic Forest's tap-for-mana ability is an activated mana ability, which doesn't go on the stack and therefore can't be targeted, countered, or otherwise responded to; it resolves immediately after activation [CR 605.3b]. The ability meets all criteria for a mana ability: it requires no target, adds mana to the player's mana pool, isn't a loyalty ability, and doesn't move cards to or from a library [CR 605.1a].",
      "sources": [
        {
          "id": "CR 605.3b",
          "title": "Rule 605.3b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "An activated mana ability doesnâ€™t go on the stack, so it canâ€™t be targeted, countered, or otherwise responded to. Rather, it resolves immediately after it is activated. (See rule 405.6c.)",
          "url": "/api/rules/605.3b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 605.1a",
          "title": "Rule 605.1a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "An activated ability is a mana ability if it meets all of the following criteria: it doesnâ€™t require a target (see rule 115.6), it could add mana to a playerâ€™s mana pool when it resolves, itâ€™s not a loyalty ability (see rule 606, â€œLoyalty Abilitiesâ€), and its cost and effect donâ€™t move any card to or from a library. Do not take into account replacement effects that may apply, other than self-replacement effects, when evaluating these criteria.",
          "url": "/api/rules/605.1a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 605.4a",
          "title": "Rule 605.4a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A triggered mana ability doesnâ€™t go on the stack, so it canâ€™t be targeted, countered, or otherwise responded to. Rather, it resolves immediately after the mana ability that triggered it, without waiting for priority.\nExample: An enchantment reads, â€œWhenever a player taps a land for mana, that player adds one mana of any type that land produced.â€ If a player taps lands for mana while casting a spell, the additional mana is added immediately and can be used to pay for the spell.",
          "url": "/api/rules/605.4a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 605.3a",
          "title": "Rule 605.3a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A player may activate an activated mana ability whenever they have priority, whenever they are casting a spell or activating an ability that requires a mana payment, or whenever a rule or effect asks for a mana payment, even if itâ€™s in the middle of casting or resolving a spell or activating or resolving an ability.",
          "url": "/api/rules/605.3a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 605.3c",
          "title": "Rule 605.3c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Once a player begins to activate a mana ability, that ability canâ€™t be activated again until it has resolved.",
          "url": "/api/rules/605.3c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 605.1b",
          "title": "Rule 605.1b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A triggered ability is a mana ability if it meets all of the following criteria: it doesnâ€™t require a target (see rule 115.6), it triggers from the activation or resolution of an activated mana ability (see rule 605.1a) or from mana being added to a playerâ€™s mana pool, and it could add mana to a playerâ€™s mana pool when it resolves.",
          "url": "/api/rules/605.1b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1",
          "title": "Rule 117.1",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Unless a spell or ability is instructing a player to take an action, which player can take actions at any given time is determined by a system of priority. The player with priority may cast spells, activate abilities, and take special actions.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2",
          "title": "Rule 117.2",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Other kinds of abilities and actions are automatically generated or performed by the game rules, or are performed by players without receiving priority.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.3",
          "title": "Rule 117.3",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Which player has priority is determined by the following rules:",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.3"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.4",
          "title": "Rule 117.4",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If all players pass in succession (that is, if all players pass without taking any actions in between passing), the spell or ability on top of the stack resolves or, if the stack is empty, the phase or step ends.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.4"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.5",
          "title": "Rule 117.5",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Each time a player would get priority, the game first performs all applicable state-based actions as a single event (see rule 704, â€œState-Based Actionsâ€), then repeats this process until no state-based actions are performed. Then triggered abilities are put on the stack (see rule 603, â€œHandling Triggered Abilitiesâ€). These steps repeat in order until no further state-based actions are performed and no abilities trigger. Then the player who would have received priority does so.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.5"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.6",
          "title": "Rule 117.6",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "In a multiplayer game using the shared team turns option, teams rather than individual players have priority. See rule 805, â€œShared Team Turns Option.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.6"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1a",
          "title": "Rule 117.1a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A player may cast an instant spell any time they have priority. A player may cast a noninstant spell during their main phase any time they have priority and the stack is empty.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1b",
          "title": "Rule 117.1b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A player may activate an activated ability any time they have priority.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1c",
          "title": "Rule 117.1c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A player may take some special actions any time they have priority. A player may take other special actions during their main phase any time they have priority and the stack is empty. See rule 116, â€œSpecial Actions.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.1d",
          "title": "Rule 117.1d",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "A player may activate a mana ability whenever they have priority, whenever they are casting a spell or activating an ability that requires a mana payment, or whenever a rule or effect asks for a mana payment (even in the middle of casting or resolving a spell or activating or resolving an ability).",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.1d"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2a",
          "title": "Rule 117.2a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Triggered abilities can trigger at any time, including while a spell is being cast, an ability is being activated, or a spell or ability is resolving. (See rule 603, â€œHandling Triggered Abilities.â€) However, nothing actually happens at the time an ability triggers. Each time a player would receive priority, each ability that has triggered but hasnâ€™t yet been put on the stack is put on the stack. See rule 117.5.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2b",
          "title": "Rule 117.2b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Static abilities continuously affect the game. Priority doesnâ€™t apply to them. (See rule 604, â€œHandling Static Abilities,â€ and rule 611, â€œContinuous Effects.â€)",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2c",
          "title": "Rule 117.2c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Turn-based actions happen automatically when certain steps or phases begin. Theyâ€™re dealt with before a player would receive priority. See rule 117.3a. Turn-based actions also happen automatically when each step and phase ends; no player receives priority afterward. See rule 703, â€œTurn-Based Actions.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2d",
          "title": "Rule 117.2d",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "State-based actions happen automatically when certain conditions are met. See rule 704. Theyâ€™re dealt with before a player would receive priority. See rule 117.5.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2d"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.2e",
          "title": "Rule 117.2e",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Resolving spells and abilities may instruct players to make choices or take actions, or may allow players to activate mana abilities. Even if a player is doing so, no player has priority while a spell or ability is resolving. See rule 608, â€œResolving Spells and Abilities.â€",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.2e"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.3a",
          "title": "Rule 117.3a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "The active player receives priority at the beginning of most steps and phases, after any turn-based actions (such as drawing a card during the draw step; see rule 703) have been dealt with and abilities that trigger at the beginning of that phase or step have been put on the stack. No player receives priority during the untap step. Players usually donâ€™t get priority during the cleanup step (see rule 514.3).",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.3a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.3b",
          "title": "Rule 117.3b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "The active player receives priority after a spell or ability (other than a mana ability) resolves.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.3b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.3c",
          "title": "Rule 117.3c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a player has priority when they cast a spell, activate an ability, or take a special action, that player receives priority afterward.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.3c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 117.3d",
          "title": "Rule 117.3d",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a player has priority and chooses not to take any actions, that player passes. If any mana is in that playerâ€™s mana pool, they announce what mana is there. Then the next player in turn order receives priority.",
          "url": "/api/rules/117.3d"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.1",
          "title": "Rule 405.1",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "When a spell is cast, the physical card is put on the stack (see rule 601.2a). When an ability is activated or triggers, it goes on top of the stack without any card associated with it (see rules 602.2a and 603.3).",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.1"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.2",
          "title": "Rule 405.2",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "The stack keeps track of the order that spells and/or abilities were added to it. Each time an object is put on the stack, itâ€™s put on top of all objects already there.",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.2"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.3",
          "title": "Rule 405.3",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If an effect puts two or more objects on the stack at the same time, those controlled by the active player are put on lowest, followed by each other playerâ€™s objects in APNAP order (see rule 101.4). If a player controls more than one of these objects, that player chooses their relative order on the stack.",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.3"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.4",
          "title": "Rule 405.4",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Each spell has all the characteristics of the card associated with it. Each activated or triggered ability thatâ€™s on the stack has the text of the ability that created it and no other characteristics. The controller of a spell is the player who cast it. The controller of an activated ability is the player who activated it. The controller of a triggered ability is the player who controlled the abilityâ€™s source when it triggered, unless itâ€™s a delayed triggered ability. To determine the controller of a delayed triggered ability, see rules 603.7dâ€“f.",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.4"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.5",
          "title": "Rule 405.5",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "When all players pass in succession, the top (last-added) spell or ability on the stack resolves. If the stack is empty when all players pass, the current step or phase ends and the next begins.",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.5"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 405.6",
          "title": "Rule 405.6",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Some things that happen during the game donâ€™t use the stack.",
          "url": "/api/rules/405.6"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.1",
          "title": "Rule 608.1",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Each time all players pass in succession, the spell or ability on top of the stack resolves. (See rule 609, â€œEffects.â€)",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.1"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.2",
          "title": "Rule 608.2",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is an instant spell, a sorcery spell, or an ability, its resolution may involve several steps. The steps described in rules 608.2a and 608.2b are followed first. The steps described in rules 608.2câ€“m are then followed as appropriate, in no specific order. The steps described in rule 608.2n and 608.2p are followed last.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.2"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3",
          "title": "Rule 608.3",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is a permanent spell, its resolution may involve several steps. The instructions in rules 608.3a and b are always performed first. Then one of the steps in rule 608.3câ€“e is performed, if appropriate.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.2f",
          "title": "Rule 608.2f",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Some spells and abilities include actions taken on multiple players and/or objects. In most cases, each such action is processed simultaneously. If the action canâ€™t be processed simultaneously, itâ€™s instead processed considering each affected player or object individually. APNAP order is used to make the primary determination of the order of those actions. Secondarily, if the action is to be taken on both a player and an object they control or on multiple objects controlled by the same player, the player who controls the resolving spell or ability chooses the relative order of those actions.\nExample: Blatant Thievery says â€œFor each opponent, gain control of target permanent that player controls.â€ As Blatant Thievery resolves, its controller gains control of all permanents chosen as targets simultaneously.\nExample: Soulfire Eruption says, in part, â€œChoose any number of target creatures, planeswalkers, and/or players. For each of them, exile the top card of your library, then Soulfire Eruption deals damage equal to that cardâ€™s mana value to that permanent or player.â€ A player casts Soulfire Eruption targeting an opponent and a creature that opponent controls. As Soulfire Eruption resolves, the player canâ€™t exile the top card of their library multiple times at the same time, so they first choose which target they are considering, then they exile the top card of their library, and finally Soulfire Eruption deals damage to that target. They then repeat this process for the remaining target.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.2f"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.2g",
          "title": "Rule 608.2g",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If an effect gives a player the option to pay mana, they may activate mana abilities before taking that action. If an effect specifically instructs or allows a player to cast a spell during resolution, they do so by following the steps in rules 601.2aâ€“i, except no player receives priority after itâ€™s cast. That spell becomes the topmost object on the stack, and the currently resolving spell or ability continues to resolve, which may include casting other spells this way. No other spells can normally be cast and no other abilities can normally be activated during resolution.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.2g"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3a",
          "title": "Rule 608.3a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving has no targets, it becomes a permanent and enters the battlefield under the control of the spellâ€™s controller.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3b",
          "title": "Rule 608.3b",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving has a target, it checks whether the target is still legal, as described in 608.2b. If a spell with an illegal target is a bestowed Aura spell (see rule 702.103e) or a mutating creature spell (see rule 702.140b), it becomes a creature spell and will resolve as described in rule 608.3a. Otherwise, the spell doesnâ€™t resolve. It is removed from the stack and put into its ownerâ€™s graveyard.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3b"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3c",
          "title": "Rule 608.3c",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is an Aura spell, it becomes a permanent and is put onto the battlefield under the control of the spellâ€™s controller attached to the player or object it was targeting.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3c"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3d",
          "title": "Rule 608.3d",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is a mutating creature spell, the object representing that spell merges with the permanent it is targeting (see rule 730, â€œMerging with Permanentsâ€).",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3d"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3e",
          "title": "Rule 608.3e",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If a permanent spell resolves but its controller canâ€™t put it onto the battlefield, that player puts it into its ownerâ€™s graveyard.\nExample: Worms of the Earth has the ability â€œLands canâ€™t enter the battlefield.â€ Clone says â€œYou may have this creature enter as a copy of any creature on the battlefield.â€ If a player casts Clone and chooses to copy Dryad Arbor (a land creature) while Worms of the Earth is on the battlefield, Clone canâ€™t enter the battlefield from the stack. Itâ€™s put into its ownerâ€™s graveyard.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3e"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3f",
          "title": "Rule 608.3f",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving is a copy of a permanent spell, it will become a token permanent as it is put onto the battlefield in any of the steps above. A token put onto the battlefield this way is no longer a copy of a spell and is not â€œcreatedâ€ for the purposes of any rules or effects that refer to creating a token.",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3f"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 608.3g",
          "title": "Rule 608.3g",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "If the object thatâ€™s resolving has a static ability that functions on the stack and creates a delayed triggered ability, that delayed triggered ability is created as that permanent is put onto the battlefield in any of the steps above. (See rules 702.109, â€œDash,â€ 702.152, â€œBlitz,â€ and 702.185, â€œWarp.â€)",
          "url": "/api/rules/608.3g"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 601.1",
          "title": "Rule 601.1",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Previously, the action of casting a spell, or casting a card as a spell, was referred to on cards as â€œplayingâ€ that spell or that card. Cards that were printed with that text have received errata in the Oracle card reference so they now refer to â€œcastingâ€ that spell or that card.",
          "url": "/api/rules/601.1"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 601.1a",
          "title": "Rule 601.1a",
          "kind": "rule",
          "text": "Some effects still refer to â€œplayingâ€ a card. â€œPlaying a cardâ€ means playing that card as a land or casting that card as a spell, whichever is appropriate.",
          "url": "/api/rules/601.1a"
        },
        {
          "id": "CR 601.2",
